    { marks: 3, q: 'Why do different persons have different notions of development? Explain with examples.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Life situations differ; people seek what is most important to them', kw: ['situation|aspiration|important'] },
        { text: 'Examples of different goals (labourer: work and wages; Punjab farmer: support prices; girl: freedom)', kw: ['wage|work', 'farmer|freedom'] },
        { text: 'Goals may conflict — dam: electricity vs displacement', kw: ['dam|conflict|destructive'] }
      ],
      model: 'Different persons have different notions of development because their life situations are different. People seek things that are most important for them and fulfil their aspirations. A landless rural labourer wants more days of work and better wages, a prosperous Punjab farmer wants higher support prices, and a girl from a rich family wants as much freedom as her brother. Goals can even conflict: industrialists want dams for electricity, but a dam may displace Adivasis. So development for one may be destructive for another.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'Besides income, what other goals do people have? Give examples.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Non-material goals: equal treatment, freedom, security, respect, no discrimination', kw: ['freedom|equal', 'security|respect|discriminat'] },
        { text: 'Women in paid work gain dignity', kw: ['women|woman', 'dignity'] },
        { text: 'Job choice depends on other factors like security, facilities, atmosphere', kw: ['job|work'] }
      ],
      model: 'Besides more income, people also seek equal treatment, freedom, security and respect of others, and they do not like to be discriminated against. These goals are as important as income. For example, if women are engaged in paid work, their dignity in the household and society increases, and a safe environment lets more women take up jobs or run a business. Similarly, before accepting a job, a person considers job security, facilities for the family, working atmosphere and the chance to learn, not only the salary.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'What is the main criterion used by the World Bank in classifying countries? What are the limitations of this criterion?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Per capita (average) income; rich ≥ US$ 12,056, low income ≤ US$ 955; India low middle income', kw: ['per capita|average income'] },
        { text: 'Limitation: averages hide disparities in distribution of income', kw: ['disparit|inequal|distribut'] },
        { text: 'Limitation: ignores health, education, freedom, environment', kw: ['health|education|literacy'] }
      ],
      model: 'The World Bank uses per capita (average) income as the main criterion in its World Development Report. In 2017, countries with per capita income of US$ 12,056 per annum and above were called rich, and those with US$ 955 or less were low-income countries; India is in the low middle income group.\nLimitations: (i) averages hide disparities — they do not show how income is distributed among people; (ii) it considers only income and ignores other important factors like health, education, freedom and a safe environment.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'Why do we use averages? Are there any limitations to their use? Illustrate with your own examples.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Averages are useful for comparing countries/states of different sizes', kw: ['compar', 'population|size'] },
        { text: 'Limitation: they hide disparities', kw: ['hide|conceal', 'disparit|inequal'] },
        { text: 'Example: Country A vs Country B both average Rs 10,000', kw: ['10,000'] }
      ],
      model: 'We use averages because they are convenient for comparing countries or states that have different population sizes; total income would not tell us what an average person earns.\nLimitation: averages hide disparities. Example: in Country A five people earn about Rs 10,000 each, while in Country B four people earn Rs 500 each and one earns Rs 48,000. Both have an average income of Rs 10,000, yet B is highly unequal. So the average does not show how income is distributed.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'Kerala, with lower per capita income, has a better human development ranking than Haryana. Hence, per capita income is not a useful criterion at all and should not be used to compare states. Do you agree? Discuss.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Kerala has lower IMR, higher literacy and attendance due to health and education facilities / PDS', kw: ['kerala', 'health|education|literacy'] },
        { text: 'Per capita income alone is not adequate; HDI is broader', kw: ['not adequate|not enough|alone|only'] },
        { text: 'But income is still useful/important — should be used along with other indicators', kw: ['useful|important', 'along with|together|other indicator|also'] }
      ],
      model: 'No, I do not fully agree. Kerala has a lower per capita income than Haryana, but it has a much lower infant mortality rate and higher literacy and net attendance ratios, because it has adequate basic health and education facilities and a well-functioning PDS. This shows that per capita income alone is not an adequate measure of development. However, income is still useful and important, because people need income to buy goods and services. So per capita income should be used along with other indicators like health and education, as the HDI does.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'Money cannot buy all the goods and services needed to live well. Explain with examples.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Some things need collective provision', kw: ['collective|public|community'] },
        { text: 'Examples: pollution-free environment, unadulterated medicines, protection from infectious diseases', kw: ['pollution', 'medicine|disease'] },
        { text: 'Public facilities like health, education and PDS are cheaper and more effective', kw: ['pds|school|health'] }
      ],
      model: 'Money in your pocket cannot buy all the goods and services that you may need to live well. Income is not a complete indicator of what citizens can use. For example, money cannot buy a pollution-free environment or ensure that you get unadulterated medicines. It cannot protect you from infectious diseases unless the whole community takes preventive steps. Such things need collective or public provision. Public facilities like government health care, schools and the PDS are cheaper and more effective when provided for everyone — as Kerala shows.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'In what respects is the criterion used by the UNDP for measuring development different from the one used by the World Bank?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'World Bank: only per capita income', kw: ['world bank', 'per capita|income'] },
        { text: 'UNDP: health (life expectancy), education (years of schooling) and per capita income in PPP US$', kw: ['life expectancy|health', 'education|schooling'] },
        { text: 'UNDP is broader — Human Development Index', kw: ['broader|wider|hdi|human development'] }
      ],
      model: 'The World Bank (World Development Report) classifies countries only on the basis of per capita income.\nThe UNDP (Human Development Report) compares countries on three things: health status (life expectancy at birth), educational levels (mean and expected years of schooling) and per capita income in PPP US$.\nSo the UNDP criterion is broader. It gives the Human Development Index (HDI), which shows the well-being of people and not just income. That is why Sri Lanka ranks above India in HDI.' },
    { marks: 3, q: 'Why is the issue of sustainability important for development?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Resources are limited; present development must not harm future generations', kw: ['future'] },
        { text: 'Groundwater overuse (one-third of country); crude oil running out', kw: ['groundwater', 'oil'] },
        { text: 'Environmental degradation does not respect boundaries', kw: ['boundar|environment'] }
      ],
      model: 'Sustainability is important because development must continue for future generations too. Resources are limited, and if we overuse them now, the present level of development cannot be maintained. Nearly one-third of India is overusing its groundwater, which is replenished by rain much more slowly. Non-renewable resources like crude oil will run out after some years. Environmental degradation also does not respect state or national boundaries, so its effects are shared by all. Therefore development must be sustainable.' },
    { marks: 4, q: 'CASE STUDY: Read the source and answer.\n"Country A: five citizens earn Rs 9,500, Rs 10,500, Rs 9,800, Rs 10,000 and Rs 10,200 a month. Country B: four citizens earn Rs 500 each and one earns Rs 48,000 a month."\n(a) What is the average income of each country? (1)\n(b) Which country has a more equitable distribution of income? (1)\n(c) What does this example tell us about the use of averages? (2)',
      keypoints: [
        { text: '(a) Rs 10,000 in both', kw: ['10,000'] },
        { text: '(b) Country A', kw: ['country a'] },
        { text: '(c) Averages are useful for comparison but hide disparities / distribution', kw: ['compar', 'hide|disparit|unequal'] }
      ],
      model: '(a) Both countries have a total income of Rs 50,000 for five people, so the average income of each is Rs 10,000.\n(b) Country A, because all its citizens earn nearly the same amount.\n(c) Averages are useful for comparing countries, but they hide disparities. Two countries with the same average may have very different (unequal) distribution of income, as in Country B, where most people are very poor.' },
    { marks: 4, q: 'CASE STUDY: Read the source and answer.\n"Among three states, Haryana has the highest per capita income and Bihar the lowest. Yet Kerala has the lowest infant mortality rate and the highest literacy rate and net attendance ratio. Kerala has adequate provision of basic health and educational facilities, and its Public Distribution System functions well."\n(a) Define net attendance ratio. (1)\n(b) Which state has the lowest infant mortality rate? (1)\n(c) Give two reasons why Kerala does better than Haryana despite lower income. (2)',
      keypoints: [
        { text: '(a) Children aged 14–15 attending school as % of all children of that age', kw: ['14', 'school'] },
        { text: '(b) Kerala', kw: ['kerala'] },
        { text: '(c) Adequate basic health and education facilities; PDS works well', kw: ['health', 'pds|public distribution'] }
      ],
      model: '(a) Net attendance ratio is the total number of children of age group 14 and 15 years attending school as a percentage of the total number of children in the same age group.\n(b) Kerala.\n(c) (i) Kerala has adequate provision of basic health and educational facilities, which lowers infant deaths and raises literacy. (ii) Its Public Distribution System (PDS) functions well, so people get food at fair prices and have better nutrition. Income alone does not decide development.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Explain the meaning of national development. How do we compare different countries? What are the limitations of using per capita income?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'National development: fair and just path for all; people have conflicting ideas', kw: ['fair|just', 'all|everyone'] },
        { text: 'Income is the most common basis; average/per capita income = total income ÷ population', kw: ['income', 'population'] },
        { text: 'World Bank classification; India low middle income', kw: ['world bank', 'low middle'] },
        { text: 'Limitation: averages hide disparities', kw: ['disparit|inequal'] },
        { text: 'Limitation: ignores health, education, environment', kw: ['health', 'education'] }
      ],
      model: 'National development means the development of the country as a whole. Since different people have different and even conflicting goals, we must think about whether there is a fair and just path for all and find better ways of development for everyone.\nComparing countries: income is the most common basis. Since populations differ, we use average or per capita income = total income of the country ÷ total population. The World Bank uses this criterion: in 2017, countries with US$ 12,056 per capita and above were rich and those with US$ 955 or less were low-income. India is in the low middle income group.\nLimitations: (i) averages hide disparities — Country A and Country B may both average Rs 10,000 while B is very unequal; (ii) income ignores health, education, freedom, security and environment — Kerala, with lower income than Haryana, has better health and education.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'What is the Human Development Report? Explain its components. Why does Sri Lanka rank better than India?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Published by UNDP; compares countries on education, health and income', kw: ['undp', 'education|schooling', 'health|life expectancy'] },
        { text: 'Life expectancy at birth defined', kw: ['life expectancy', 'length of life|expected'] },
        { text: 'Years of schooling (mean and expected)', kw: ['years of schooling'] },
        { text: 'GNI per capita in PPP US$', kw: ['ppp|purchasing power'] },
        { text: 'Sri Lanka, though smaller, is better in health and education; size or income alone does not decide', kw: ['sri lanka', 'small'] }
      ],
      model: 'The Human Development Report is published by the UNDP (United Nations Development Programme). It compares countries based on the educational levels of people, their health status and per capita income, and ranks them by the Human Development Index (HDI).\nComponents:\n• Life expectancy at birth — the average expected length of life of a person at the time of birth (health).\n• Mean years of schooling of people aged 25 and above, and expected years of schooling for children (education).\n• Gross national income per capita in PPP US$ — purchasing power parity makes incomes comparable.\nSri Lanka, a much smaller country, ranks far better than India because its people have higher life expectancy and more years of schooling, due to better public health and education. India still ranks above Bangladesh, Nepal, Myanmar and Pakistan. This shows that size or income alone does not decide human development.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'What is sustainable development? Explain with examples of groundwater and crude oil. How is Gandhiji\'s statement "The Earth has enough resources to meet the needs of all but not enough to satisfy the greed of even one person" relevant?',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'Meaning: development that meets present needs without harming future generations', kw: ['future'] },
        { text: 'Groundwater: renewable, replenished by rain, but overused in one-third of India', kw: ['groundwater', 'one-third|rain'] },
        { text: 'Crude oil: non-renewable, reserves running out', kw: ['crude oil|oil', 'non-renewable'] },
        { text: 'Environmental degradation crosses boundaries', kw: ['boundar'] },
        { text: 'Gandhiji: greed causes overuse; use resources wisely and share fairly', kw: ['greed', 'need'] }
      ],
      model: 'Sustainable development is development that meets the needs of the present without reducing the ability of future generations to meet their needs.\nGroundwater is a renewable resource replenished by rain, but nearly one-third of India is overusing it — in Punjab and western UP, hard-rock areas of central and south India, some coastal areas and growing cities. If we draw water faster than rain replenishes it, it will run out.\nCrude oil is a non-renewable resource. Its reserves are limited and will be exhausted in some decades at the present rate.\nEnvironmental degradation does not respect national or state boundaries, so it is a common concern.\nGandhiji\'s statement is very relevant: nature can satisfy every person\'s basic need, but greed — wasteful consumption and overuse of water, oil and forests by a few — destroys resources and creates inequality. We must use resources wisely and share them fairly.' },
    { marks: 5, q: 'Explain with suitable examples why income is not the only indicator of development. Refer to public facilities and the Body Mass Index.',
      keypoints: [
        { text: 'People want non-material goals: freedom, equality, security, respect', kw: ['freedom|equal|security|respect'] },
        { text: 'Haryana vs Kerala: higher income but worse IMR and literacy', kw: ['haryana', 'kerala'] },
        { text: 'Money cannot buy pollution-free environment, unadulterated medicines; public facilities and PDS', kw: ['pollution', 'pds|public distribution'] },
        { text: 'BMI = weight ÷ height²; below 18.5 undernourished, above 25 overweight', kw: ['18.5', '25'] },
        { text: 'Undernourishment exists despite enough food — income/access matters', kw: ['undernourish'] }
      ],
      model: 'Income is important, but it is not the only indicator of development.\n• People also want freedom, equal treatment, security and respect, which money cannot measure.\n• Haryana has a higher per capita income than Kerala, but Kerala has a far lower infant mortality rate and higher literacy and attendance.\n• Money cannot buy a pollution-free environment, unadulterated medicines or protection from infectious diseases. These need public facilities. Kerala provides basic health and education, and its PDS works well, so its people are healthier.\n• Body Mass Index (BMI) = weight in kg ÷ (height in metres)². A BMI below 18.5 means undernourished and above 25 means overweight. Many adults in India are undernourished even though the country has enough food, because many cannot afford it or lack access to health care and a working PDS.\nSo health, education and public facilities must be measured along with income.' }
